---
// src/pages/share/washington-puppets/sources.astro
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Sources — Washington Puppets — Skovgard 2026"
  description="Public sources supporting the Washington Puppets message: Wyoming Statute W.S. 22-25-104, the Wyoming GOP's federal lawsuit, and the Candidate Vetting Committee process."
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="pt-20">

    <!-- Breadcrumb -->
    <div class="bg-wy-bone border-b border-gray-200 py-3 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest">
        <a href="/share" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-wy-ember transition-colors">All messages</a>
        <span class="text-gray-300">/</span>
        <a href="/share/washington-puppets" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-wy-ember transition-colors">Washington Puppets</a>
        <span class="text-gray-300">/</span>
        <span class="text-wy-charcoal">Sources</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="bg-wy-charcoal py-14 px-4 text-center">
      <p class="text-wy-sandstone font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-xs mb-3">Research Notes</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-serif font-extrabold text-wy-bone mb-4 leading-tight">
        Washington Puppets
      </h1>
      <p class="text-wy-bone/70 text-lg max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Sources supporting the claims in the share message.
        This is a research aid, not a legal opinion.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Preamble -->
    <section class="bg-wy-bone border-b border-gray-200 py-10 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl px-6 py-5 space-y-3">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>About this page.</strong> This page documents the public sources behind the
            Washington Puppets message: Wyoming's statutory restriction on party spending in
            primary elections, the Wyoming Republican Party's federal lawsuit challenging that
            restriction, and the Candidate Vetting Committee notice referenced in the message.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Important limitation.</strong> The Candidate Vetting Committee notice is a
            campaign-held document — an email sent directly to statewide candidates. It has not
            been independently published online, so no public link is available for it below. It
            is described here from the original document, with personal contact information
            omitted. Court filings and any future ruling on the pending lawsuit may change the
            legal status of this dispute.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Main content -->
    <article class="bg-wy-bone py-12 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto sources-content">

        <!-- Core issue -->
        <h2>Core issue</h2>
        <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          The message argues that a Wyoming primary election should belong to the voters — not to
          a party committee acting before voters have a chance to weigh in. It rests on four
          public facts: the statutory restriction on party spending in a primary, the party's
          pending federal lawsuit against that restriction, the party's hiring of outside counsel
          for that lawsuit, and the Candidate Vetting Committee's 24-hour response window.
        </p>

        <!-- Wyoming law -->
        <h2>Wyoming law at issue</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The relevant statute is <strong>W.S. 22-25-104</strong>, titled
          <em>Restriction on party funds in primary elections</em>.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="statute-block">
          "No political party funds shall be expended directly or indirectly in the aid of the
          nomination of any one person as against another person of the same political party
          running in the primary election."
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          W.S. 22-25-104 — Wyoming Secretary of State Election Code PDF; Wyoming Legislature
          Title 22 PDF.
        </p>

        <!-- Wyoming constitution -->
        <h2>Wyoming constitutional standard</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Wyoming Constitution, Article 1, Section 27, <em>Elections free and equal</em>, protects
          the "untrammeled exercise of the right of suffrage." Whether a particular party action
          violates this provision is a legal question for a court — the text supplies the
          constitutional principle invoked in the public argument, not a ruling on this dispute.
        </p>

        <!-- Timeline -->
        <h2>Public timeline and context</h2>
        <div class="timeline-list mb-10">
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">April 23–25, 2026</span>
            <p>
              The Wyoming Republican Party held its 2026 State Convention. WyoFile reported that
              the party adopted bylaws establishing a candidate-vetting, endorsement, and
              financial-support framework, and that Chairman Bryan Miller said the party had
              hired the Washington, D.C. law firm Barr &amp; Klein for a constitutional challenge,
              to be handled "effectively pro bono."
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">June 4, 2026</span>
            <p>
              The Wyoming Republican Party filed a federal lawsuit challenging W.S. 22-25-104,
              naming Secretary of State Chuck Gray in his official capacity. The party argues the
              restriction violates its First Amendment rights.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <span class="timeline-date">July 17, 2026</span>
            <p>
              Ginger Bennett, Executive Director of the Wyoming Republican Party, sent statewide
              candidates a notice describing a 13-member Candidate Vetting Committee, its approved
              criteria and recommendation process, and a 24-hour window for a candidate response.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Claims table -->
        <h2>Key claims and source support</h2>
        <div class="claims-table" role="table" aria-label="Claims and source support">
          <div class="claims-header" role="row">
            <span role="columnheader">Public claim</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Best source</span>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              A 13-member Candidate Vetting Committee gave candidates 24 hours to respond before
              a recommendation.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              Ginger Bennett email to statewide candidates, July 17, 2026. Campaign-held document;
              not published online.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              Wyoming law restricts party funds from aiding one same-party primary candidate over
              another.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              W.S. 22-25-104. Wyoming Secretary of State Election Code PDF; Wyoming Legislature
              Title 22 PDF; Justia mirror.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              The party hired a Washington, D.C. law firm and filed a federal lawsuit challenging
              that statute.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              WyoFile reporting on the state convention (Barr &amp; Klein, Chairman Bryan Miller)
              and on the June 4, 2026 lawsuit filing.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="claims-row" role="row">
            <div role="cell">
              Until a court rules otherwise, the statute remains in force.
            </div>
            <div role="cell">
              General legal principle — a challenged statute remains enforceable law unless and
              until enjoined or struck down. Not a claim about how the court will rule.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Source list -->
        <h2>Primary sources</h2>

        <div class="source-list">

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming Secretary of State: 2026 Election Code PDF
              <span class="source-desc">Official state election-law source — W.S. 22-25-104</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://sos.wyo.gov/forms/Publications/ElectionCode_8.5x11.pdf"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              sos.wyo.gov — Election Code PDF
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Official source for W.S. 22-25-104, the statute restricting party funds in primary
              elections. Statute text appears on page 200 of the 2026 Election Code.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming Legislature: Title 22 PDF
              <span class="source-desc">Official state statutes source — election laws including W.S. 22-25-104</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://wyoleg.gov/statutes/compress/title22.pdf"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyoleg.gov — Title 22 PDF
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Legislature-hosted source for Title 22. Use as a second official legal source or
              backup for the Secretary of State Election Code PDF.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Justia: W.S. 22-25-104
              <span class="source-desc">Readable statute mirror — Restriction on party funds in primary elections</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://law.justia.com/codes/wyoming/title-22/chapter-25/section-22-25-104/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              law.justia.com — WY Stat Section 22-25-104
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Plain web version of the statute. Use alongside an official Wyoming state source.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Wyoming Legislature: Constitution of the State of Wyoming (PDF)
              <span class="source-desc">Article 1, Section 27 — Elections free and equal</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://sos.wyo.gov/Forms/Publications/WYConstitution.pdf"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              sos.wyo.gov — Wyoming Constitution PDF
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Official source for the "untrammeled exercise of the right of suffrage" language.
              Supplies constitutional principle, not a ruling on this dispute.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              WyoFile — Wyoming GOP sues state over pre-primary endorsements
              <span class="source-desc">Independent Wyoming news report — federal lawsuit challenging W.S. 22-25-104</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://wyofile.com/wyoming-gop-sues-state-challenges-constitutionality-of-ban-on-pre-primary-candidate-endorsements/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyofile.com — Wyoming GOP sues state
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Reports the June 4, 2026 federal lawsuit naming Secretary of State Chuck Gray in his
              official capacity.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              WyoFile — Wyoming Republican Party plans to buck law and endorse candidates ahead of critical primary
              <span class="source-desc">Independent Wyoming news report — bylaws, vetting process, and Washington counsel</span>
            </p>
            <a href="https://wyofile.com/wyoming-republican-party-plans-to-buck-law-and-endorse-candidates-ahead-of-critical-primary/"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="source-link">
              wyofile.com — Wyoming Republican Party plans to buck law...
            </a>
            <p class="source-note">
              Reports the April 2026 convention bylaws establishing the vetting and endorsement
              framework, and Chairman Bryan Miller's statement that the party hired Barr &amp; Klein,
              a Washington, D.C. law firm, to be handled "effectively pro bono."
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              Candidate Vetting Committee notice
              <span class="source-desc">Campaign-held document — not published online</span>
            </p>
            <span class="source-link todo-link">
              No public link available — email from Ginger Bennett, Executive Director, Wyoming
              Republican Party, to statewide candidates, dated July 17, 2026
            </span>
            <p class="source-note">
              Describes a 13-member Candidate Vetting Committee, its approved criteria, its
              recommendation process, and a 24-hour window for a candidate response. Personal
              contact information has been omitted.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="source-item">
            <p class="source-label">
              George Orwell, <em>Nineteen Eighty-Four</em>, Part Three, Chapter Three
              <span class="source-desc">Literary quotation, not evidence of any Wyoming official's or organization's motives</span>
            </p>
            <p class="source-note" style="margin-top:0;">
              "The Party told you to reject the evidence of your eyes and ears." Page numbers vary
              by edition.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <!-- Notes for responsible use -->
        <h2>Notes for responsible use</h2>
        <ul class="responsible-list">
          <li>W.S. 22-25-104 is a statute, not a court ruling. Describe it as Wyoming law restricting party funds — not as a ruling on the pending lawsuit.</li>
          <li>The federal lawsuit is pending. Describe the party's legal challenge as a filed complaint, not a resolved question — the statute remains in force unless and until a court says otherwise.</li>
          <li>Distinguish the party's bylaws and vetting process (adopted at convention, reported by WyoFile) from the statute itself (passed law, distinct legal document).</li>
          <li>The Candidate Vetting Committee notice is a private, campaign-held communication. Do not represent it as a public document, and omit personal contact information in any further use.</li>
          <li>The Orwell quotation is literary commentary used to frame an argument about institutional pressure — not a factual claim about the motives of any named person or organization.</li>
        </ul>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Back CTA -->
    <div class="bg-white py-12 px-4 border-t border-gray-200 text-center">
      <a href="/share/washington-puppets"
         class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 bg-wy-ember text-wy-bone font-bold rounded-lg hover:bg-wy-ember-dark transition-colors text-sm">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
        </svg>
        Back to Washington Puppets
      </a>
    </div>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .sources-content { line-height: 1.75; color: #2b2b2b; }
  .sources-content h2 {
    font-family: 'Bitter', serif;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    color: #2b2b2b;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #c68a4a;
  }
  .sources-content h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }

  .statute-block {
    background: #fefce8;
    border-left: 4px solid #c68a4a;
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #2b2b2b;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* Timeline */
  .timeline-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
    border-left: 3px solid #c68a4a;
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin-left: 8px;
  }
  .timeline-item {
    position: relative;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .timeline-item::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -27px;
    top: 6px;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #c68a4a;
    border: 2px solid #f1ece1;
  }
  .timeline-item:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }
  .timeline-date {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: #c68a4a;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .timeline-item p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #374151;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Claims table */
  .claims-table {
    border: 1px solid #ddd8ce;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .claims-header {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 3fr;
    background: #2b2b2b;
    gap: 0;
  }
  .claims-header span {
    padding: 10px 16px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #f1ece1;
  }
  .claims-header span:first-child { border-right: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.12); }
  .claims-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 3fr;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd8ce;
  }
  .claims-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .claims-row div {
    padding: 14px 16px;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #2b2b2b;
  }
  .claims-row div:first-child {
    background: #f1ece1;
    border-right: 1px solid #ddd8ce;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .claims-row div:last-child { background: #fafaf8; }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .claims-header { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .claims-header span:first-child { border-right: none; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.12); }
    .claims-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .claims-row div:first-child { border-right: none; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd8ce; }
  }

  /* Source list */
  .source-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
    border: 1px solid #e5e2da;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .source-item {
    background: #fff;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e2da;
  }
  .source-item:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .source-label {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: #0f2742;
    margin: 0 0 4px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
  }
  .source-desc {
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #6b7280;
  }
  .source-link {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #b22234;
    text-decoration: none;
    word-break: break-all;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .source-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .todo-link {
    color: #6b7280;
    font-style: italic;
    cursor: default;
  }
  .source-note {
    margin: 4px 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #6b7280;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Responsible use */
  .responsible-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .responsible-list li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: #374151;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .responsible-list li::before {
    content: "•";
    color: #c68a4a;
    font-weight: 700;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }
</style>
